If the thieves don't start appearing often before, say, level five,
then the chances of his escaping the dungeon are actually rather slim.

Once the item is swiped, the thief thinks only of getting off the
level, not of getting out of the dungeon.  He would go to whatever he
finds first or knows is closest, the up or down stairs (or maybe even
a trap door or hole).  Forgetting the stairs to the mines (which would
make the "big" escape even less likely), the thief would have only a
50% chance of picking the upstairs on each level.

If the player single-minded attempted to hunt down the thief he'd get
a chance at the upstairs each time, and thus *eventually* escape the
dungeon, but the player has probably caught him by that time.  Unlike
nymphs, these thieves would have no means of teleportation other than
the scroll they begin with, and monsters off the current level don't
get turns so he wouldn't be able to escape so long as the player
doesn't enter his level.

This might be interesting in conjunction with the Grudge patch (which
I think is a great idea BTW, anything that increases the perception
that the dungeon has a life beyond stopping the player is a big plus).
 The minetown guards would probably have a grudge against them, making
one possible strategy for handling thieves to try to corral them
towards Minetown.

